in the last episode we looked at how to  store our unstructured data  in this episode we'll learn how to run  custom training jobs on vertex ai i'm  nikita and this is prototype to  production where we go from notebook  code to a deployed model in the cloud  [Music]  you might be wondering why do i need a  training service when i can just run  model training directly in my notebook  well for models that take a long time to  train a notebook isn't always the most  convenient option and if you're building  an application with ml it's unlikely  you'll only need to train your model  once  over time you'll want to retrain your  model to make sure it stays fresh and  keeps producing valuable results  manually executing the cells of your  notebook might be the right option when  you're getting started with a new ml  problem but when you want to automate  experimentation at scale or retrain  models for a production application a  managed ml training option will make  things much easier not to mention vertex  ci training comes with additional  features like hyper parameter tuning  distributed training support and  integration with other parts of vertex  ai  running a custom training job on vertex  ai is done with containers  don't panic if you don't know a whole  lot about containers or docker i'm going  to show you exactly what you need to  know  containers are packages of your  application code which in this case is  our training code  together with dependencies like specific  versions of libraries required to run  your code in addition to helping with  dependency management containers are  able to run virtually anywhere allowing  for increased portability  docker is the tool we'll use to create  these containers vertex ai training  provides a set of pre-built containers  if one of these containers meets your  needs in other words has all the  libraries needed for your use case you  can provide your training code as a  python source distribution and vertex ai  will manage the container for you  but if your training application  requires libraries that are not included  in these images or it contains code  that's not written in python or maybe  you just want better control over the  dependencies you'll want to use a custom  image and that's what i'll show you next  now our flowers model only requires  tensorflow so we can actually just use  the pre-built tensorflow image  but when it's time for you to run your  own experiments you might find yourself  with the use case that's a little more  complicated  plus knowing some basics of how to  containerize code with docker is a great  skill for anyone doing machine learning  here's a quick summary of the steps that  we'll take to run our training job  first we'll export our notebook to a  python file  then we'll update our code so that we  read the data in from cloud storage and  save the model out to cloud storage  next we'll containerize our code with  docker and lastly we'll launch a custom  training job our code is all in this  notebook right here in workbench but we  want to work with the python file  instead  we can do this conversion with the nb  convert tool from the terminal  we execute the command jupyter nbconvert  then the name of our notebook dash dash  to python and we see that we now have a  file called task.pi which is the python  file equivalent of our notebook  recall that in the previous episode we  uploaded the flowers dataset to a bucket  called prototype to production bucket  in our training code we want to read in  data directly from that bucket  using the cloud storage fuse tool  training jobs on vertex ai can access  data on cloud storage as files in the  local file system  when you start a custom training job the  job sees a directory backslash gcs which  stands for google cloud storage  that contains all of your cloud storage  buckets as subdirectories  not only does this make it easy to  access your data but it also provides  high throughput for large file  sequential reads so to access the images  in our bucket all we need to do on our  code is set the path of our data  directory to be slash gcs slash the name  of our bucket  flower photos  but we don't just want to read data from  cloud storage we also want to make sure  our trained model is saved to cloud  storage that way we can access it later  for deployment and predictions  you might be used to calling model.save  and passing in a local path  when we launch this training job it's  going to run on a machine we won't have  access to after the job completes  so we don't want model.save to be a  local path instead we want it to be a  path in cloud storage  so let's update our model.save path to  the name of our bucket slash model  output which will update our save model  artifacts to the same bucket in a  different directory  okay our training code is ready so it's  time to containerize it let's return to  the terminal and structure our  application  first i'll create a directory called  flowers and within that a directory  called trainer  trainer is where all of our training  code will go so i'll move the task.pi  file there then from the root of the  flower directory we'll create our docker  file let's review what we just did our  application looks something like this  remember all of our training code is in  the trainer directory in the task.pi  file  in this example we only have one python  file in our trainer directory but for a  more realistic example you would likely  have additional files  maybe you've got one called data.pi  which handles data preprocessing and one  called model.pi which just has the model  code etc in our docker file we'll write  a series of commands which is what the  vertex ai training service will execute  let's take a deeper look at the syntax  the from command specifies the base  container image which is the parent  container that the container we create  will be built on top of  as our base container image we'll use  the tensorflow enterprise gpu docker  image since it comes with all of the  packages we need  the workdir command specifies the  directory in the container where  subsequent instructions are run  the copy command copies the trainer code  to the docker container image and if you  wanted to add any additional libraries  you could use the run command to pip  install but we don't need anything  additional for our example here  lastly the entry point command sets up  the entry point to invoke the trainer  this is what we'll run when we start our  training job in our case that's  executing the task.pi file and that's it  with this formula you know enough to  start writing dockerfiles for your  training code now that the dockerfile  has been written we need to build our  container and push it to artifact  registry which is a place for storing  container images in google cloud  we'll start by defining the path and  artifact registry where we want to store  the container image  then we can call docker build  and when our image is built we'll push  it to artifact registry  if you missed any of these commands  don't worry i've written them all out in  the codelab linked below  once the image is pushed we'll be able  to see it in the console under artifact  registry  again this container image has all of  our training code and the dependencies  that we need  so now we're ready to launch our  training job  under the training section of the  console select create  select custom training advanced as your  training method  give your model a name  in the container settings select custom  container and then we'll add the path to  our container in artifact registry  we specify our compute  and we'll skip the prediction container  step for now and do that in the next  episode  finally we click start training  if you'd like to learn how to launch  training jobs using the python sdk  instead of the ui check out the code lab  linked below  under the custom jobs tab we can track  the status of our training job we can  click on the name to see the details on  the configuration  and when it's finished we can see the  logs  we'll also see the saved model artifacts  here in our cloud storage bucket  in this episode we talked about the  benefits of a managed training service  and walk through the steps required to  train custom models on vertex ai in the  next episode we'll use this model to get  low latency predictions with the vertex  ai prediction service be sure to check  out the code lab linked below which  provides detailed instructions on  everything we covered today  and don't forget to like and subscribe  for more great content and i'll see you  next time
 

